-<h3 id="description">Description</h3>
-<p>
-<code>RsaPrivateKey</code> private-key construction, validation, and serialization can panic across three
-classes of inputs, as well as when re-encoding certain valid keys:</p>
-<ul>
-<li>
-<code>RsaPrivateKey::from_components</code> and PKCS#1 or PKCS#8 private-key decoding (DER or PEM) can panic
-during validation when a supplied factor equals 1, because <code>RsaPrivateKey::validate()</code> let a
-factor of 1 reach a later check that panicked.</li>
-<li>
-<code>RsaPrivateKey::from_primes</code> and <code>RsaPrivateKey::from_p_q</code> can panic during construction when a
-supplied factor is 0, 1, or even.</li>
-<li>Prime recovery in <code>RsaPrivateKey::from_components</code> can panic when the private exponent is 0.</li>
-<li>For a key, including a valid one, whose first prime occupies more limbs than the second,
-<code>crt_coefficient</code>, <code>to_pkcs1_der</code>, <code>to_pkcs8_der</code>, and <code>serde</code> serialization can panic.</li>
-</ul>
-<p>An application that imports untrusted private keys or factors, or re-encodes such keys, can lose a
-worker, or the whole process when built with <code>panic = &quot;abort&quot;</code>. Public-key parsing is not affected.</p>
-<p>Fixed in 0.10.4, which rejects factors below 2, even factors, repeated factors, and a zero private
-exponent during prime recovery, and computes the CRT coefficient and re-encodes valid two-prime
-keys when the first prime occupies more limbs than the second.</p>
-<p>If you cannot upgrade to 0.10.4, isolate the entire import, construction, CRT calculation, or
-serialization in a separate process and treat an abnormal exit as an operation failure, or use
-<code>catch_unwind</code> around the entire operation when panics unwind.</p>
